{"visualPremise":"Flat 2D stickman-explainer visuals that stage a short visual investigation: drop the viewer into imagined Viking-action, cut to archaeological finds, then to Bronze Age ritual objects and 19th-century theatre, and close in a modern museum/merchandise context.","visualTone":"playful but evidence-driven: visceral and dramatic in the opening 'you charging' beat, sober and slightly skeptical when examining finds, gently ironic during the Wagner/theatre beat, and quietly corrective in the closer.","cast":[{"id":"hero","role":"viewer_viking — the second-person avatar placed in imagined Viking scenes (the 'you' who charges with horns in the opening)."},{"id":"viewer_viking","role":"the viewer (second-person Viking-era avatar) used in the opening fantasy and in accurate archaeological contrasts."},{"id":"archaeologist_modern","role":"a modern archaeologist explaining finds and showing a helmet under glass."},{"id":"bronze_age_priest","role":"a Bronze Age ritual figure to illustrate the Veksø helmets' original ceremonial context."},{"id":"wagner_theatre_designer","role":"19th-century opera/theatre costume designer and the theatre crowd used to show the origin of the dramatic stage look."},{"id":"museum_curator","role":"modern museum curator/label-placer guiding viewers past a plain helmet under glass and a display of ritual objects."},{"id":"present_day_consumer","role":"modern public / merch user to show postcards, NFL logos, and lunchboxes using the horned image."},{"id":"viking_raider_generic","role":"generic Viking raider archetype used in quick background shots of raids or shieldwalls (always historically conservative)."},{"id":"theatrical_horned_helmet","role":"a prop archetype: the dramatic horned helmet used in fantasy/theatre/advertising shots (explicitly fictional within episode)."}],"settings":[{"id":"setting_fantasy_viking_charge_hillside_field_shi","label":"fantasy_viking_charge (hillside/field shieldwall)"},{"id":"setting_archaeological_dig_and_burial_mound_inte","label":"archaeological_dig and burial mound interior"},{"id":"setting_bronze_age_ritual_clearing","label":"bronze_age_ritual clearing"},{"id":"setting_19th_century_theatre_stage_backstage","label":"19th_century_theatre stage/backstage"},{"id":"setting_modern_museum_gallery_and_merchandising_","label":"modern_museum gallery and merchandising table"}],"props":[{"id":"gjermundbu_helmet","label":"Gjermundbu helmet: a plain iron rounded dome with visible rim rivets spaced evenly, a single integrated spectacle-like eye/nasal guard (two cheek flanges joined over the nose area) and no horn sockets or rivet patterns for horn attachments; surface solid flat iron color (hex #4A4A4A). Mounted on a simple foam stand inside a glass case for museum shots."},{"id":"vekso_helmet","label":"Veksø bronze helmet: thin decorative bronze cap with two long gently curving horns attached, horn tips capped with small circular discs. Bronze color warm (hex #B6782B) with pale horn color (hex #F2E6D1). Design reads as ceremonial headdress, not protective armor."},{"id":"viking_shield","label":"Round wooden shield: solid circular disk with visible stave lines simplified to two or three radial marks, central iron boss (hex #4A4A4A), outer rim banded in darker brown (hex #6B4A2A). Strapped to left arm of viewer and raider archetypes."},{"id":"theatrical_postcard","label":"Modern postcard/lunchbox image: stylized dramatic horned-helmet figure in bold saturated colors (deep red background hex #B22222, horned helmet per 'theatrical_horned_helmet'), labeled with a small price tag icon to indicate merch. Must be captioned visually as 'souvenir'."}],"graphicLanguage":{"comparisonLayouts":"Two-up comparisons: left panel = 'archaeological reality' (Gjermundbu helmet, label, sober palette), right panel = 'popular image' (horned theatrical helmet, saturated palette). Each panel uses the same scale and a short fact-blurb beneath.","timelineConventions":"Simple flat horizontal bar with spaced era-color fills: bronze ochre for Bronze Age, muted green for Viking Age, crimson for 19th-century, cool gray for modern era. Use consistent typographic weight for date numbers.","symbolicMetaphorLanguage":"Use the 'stage spotlight' icon or small vignette to signal theatrical/fictional sources; use a small magnifying-glass icon for archaeological evidence. Use a dotted-line 'absence' halo to show the lack of surviving organic material when discussing preservation caveat."},"factualVisualConstraints":["Viking-age helmets shown as iron domes with nasal/spectacle guards (Gjermundbu-type) and no horn attachments.","Veksø helmets must be shown as Bronze Age ceremonial thin-bronze headdresses with long curved horns and small disc tips.","No contemporary Viking-age textual or carved depiction of horned battle helmets should be visually suggested in saga/carving cutaways."]}

CALLBACK PLAN: planted detail "two long curved horns" — plant in segment seg1, payoff in segment seg14.

